====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To grow [[#Adjective|plump]]; to [[swell]] [[out]].
#: ''Her cheeks have '''plumped'''.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make plump; to fill (out) or support; often with ''up''.
#: ''to '''plump''' oysters or scallops by placing them in fresh or brackish water''
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church}}
#*: to '''plump''' up the hollowness of their history with improbable miracles
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cast]] or let drop all at once, suddenly and heavily.
#: ''to '''plump''' a stone into water''
#* '''1859''', {{w|Charles Dickens}}, ''{{w|A Tale of Two Cities}}''
#*: Although Miss Pross, through her long association with a French family, might have known as much of their language as of her own, if she had had a mind, she had no mind in that direction {{...}} So her manner of marketing was to '''plump''' a noun-substantive at the head of a shopkeeper without any introduction in the nature of an article {{...}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To give a [[plumper]] (kind of vote).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give (a vote), as a plumper.
# {{indtr|en|for}} To favor or decide in favor of something.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Economist| year=2014| title=Brazil in a nutshell|pageurl=http://www.economist.com/news/americas/21600983-brazilian-workers-are-gloriously-unproductive-economy-grow-they-must-snap-out| passage=A recent poll by the New York Times found that although most Brazilians '''plump''' for arch-rival Argentina as the team they most want to lose, the second-biggest group want Brazil itself to stumble.}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|plumpen}}, akin to {{cog|dum|plompen}}, {{cog|gml|plumpen}}, {{cog|de|plumpfen}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[drop]] or [[fall]] suddenly or heavily, all at once.
#* '''September 24, 1712''', {{w|The Spectator (1711)|The Spectator}} No. 492, ''letter from a prude''
#*: Dulcissa '''plumps''' into a chair.

